WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Authentication proves who a user is, while authorization decides what that user can do.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.980
Many serious access control failures happen after login,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.400
when the application trusts a role, identifier, route,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.820
or client-side decision that should have been enforced on the server.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.360
Privilege escalation occurs when a user gains abilities meant for a higher-privileged

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.300
account.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.280
Vertical escalation might let a normal user perform administrator functions,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.820
while horizontal escalation might let one customer view or change another customer's

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.000
records.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:36.340
Insecure direct object references happen when an application exposes record identifiers,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.280
file names, tenant IDs,

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.060
or other object references without checking whether the current user is allowed to access

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.640
the target object.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.780
APIs require the same access control discipline as browser workflows.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.880
A hidden button or disabled menu item is not a security boundary if the underlying API

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.660
accepts unauthorized requests.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.880
Deny by default and require explicit authorization decisions.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.300
Perform server-side checks for every sensitive action and object.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.080
Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrative functions.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:15.020
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include AC-2 - Account Management;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.200
AC-3 - Access Enforcement; AC-6 - Least Privilege;

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.700
IA-2 - Identification and Authentication.
